{"entries":[{"entry_key":"CLM_1","claim":"An idempotent operation satisfies f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application converges to the same state as a single application. A safe operation merely guarantees that retries do not violate system invariants—it need not produce identical state, only correct state.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_1","ART_7","ART_13"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_2","claim":"Idempotency constrains the operation's effect on state (f(f(x))=f(x)), while safety constrains the operation's effect on correctness under retries (no invariant violation). For pure functions these coincide, but for stateful appends they diverge.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_14","ART_17"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_3","claim":"The append operation is inherently non-idempotent: appending event E twice yields [E,E], not [E], violating f(f(x))=f(x) by construction.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_1","ART_13","ART_14"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_4","claim":"The append-only event log requires safety of its writers, not idempotency, because append is intrinsically non-idempotent yet the system survives at-least-once delivery if duplicate entries carry deterministic deduplication keys and are filtered during replay.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_1","ART_2","ART_7","ART_13","ART_14"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_5","claim":"Both properties are required but at different architectural layers: the writer must be safe (retry attempts are harmless or detectably redundant), and the replay/deduplication layer must be idempotent in its effect on derived state. The log requires safety of its writers and idempotency of its readers, not idempotency of its writers.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_3"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_6","claim":"The log actually requires idempotency of writers, not merely safety, because deterministic replay demands that the replayed state be exactly identical to the state that would have been produced without retries. Safety, which permits state divergence as long as invariants hold, is insufficient for deterministic replay.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_4","ART_9"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_7","claim":"The distinction between idempotent and safe is a false dichotomy for append-only logs. The real requirement is commutativity with respect to the deduplication function: D(log+[E])=D(log+[E,E])=D(D(log+[E])+[E]). The log requires writers whose operations commute under D.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_5"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_8","claim":"Kafka's idempotent producer is actually safety achieved via Producer ID, sequence numbers, and transactional IDs that allow the broker to deduplicate. The append to the log is never idempotent; it is the producer-broker protocol that is safe. The log requires safety of the writer-broker protocol, and idempotency is an emergent property of the consumer's state machine.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_6"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_9","claim":"Per RFC 9110 §9.2.1, 'safe' means semantically read-only (no side effects) and 'idempotent' means repeated identical invocations have the same effect as a single one (side effects permitted). An append-only event log's writers are neither safe nor idempotent in this strict sense; what the log requires is a weaker informal 'retry-safe' property that neither RFC term captures.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_8"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_10","claim":"The distinction is a category error in the context of append-only logs: idempotency and safety are properties of operations, but the append-only log requires a property of the delivery protocol—namely, that retried writes are distinguishable from original writes by the replay layer through metadata (delivery IDs, producer epochs, sequence numbers). Writers need only attach this metadata; they need not themselves be idempotent or safe.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_11"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_11","claim":"The log requires what is best called 'effectively idempotent' writers—writers that attach deterministic identity to each event so that the composite operation of 'ensure-this-event-is-in-the-log' is idempotent even though the primitive 'append-bytes' is not. The published distinction maps to the difference between the primitive and the composite operation.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_16"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_12","claim":"What the log requires is 'commutative idempotency' of the processing function, not of the writers. Writers need only be safe (retries don't corrupt), while the processing function applied during replay must be both idempotent and commutative so that duplicate and reordered entries converge to the same replayed state—the replay function must be a semilattice fold.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_12"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_13","claim":"Through the safety-vs-liveness lens from distributed systems theory: a safety property asserts that 'something bad never happens' (no incorrect state is reached), while idempotency is a specific structural guarantee about operation semantics. The log requires the safety property—corrupted replay never arises from duplicate appends—because duplicate entries are not themselves a correctness violation if the replay engine filters them.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_15"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_14","claim":"The log requires idempotent writers, not safe ones, if one defines the writer's operation at the correct abstraction level: the operation is 'ensure this logical event is durably recorded,' which IS idempotent because ensuring recording twice leaves the same logical state as ensuring it once, achieved through deduplication keys.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_18"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_15","claim":"Counterexample approach: (a) a writer appending a random UUID each time—neither idempotent nor safe under retry; (b) a writer appending a deterministic content hash—safe (duplicates detectable and removable) but still not idempotent; (c) a writer that checks the log for an existing entry before appending—idempotent in effect but introduces a read path contradicting append-only discipline. The log requires property (b): safety through deterministic identity.","claim_class":"surviving_conjecture","formal_artifact_handles":["ART_2","ART_10"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_16","claim":"The primitive append operation cannot itself satisfy idempotency, because appending E twice produces [E,E] which is a different state from appending E once [E], directly violating the idempotency condition f(f(x))=f(x).","claim_class":"supported_inference","premise_keys":["CLM_1","CLM_3"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_17","claim":"Conflict between the category-error framing (protocol-level distinguishability is the real invariant, not an operation property of writers) and the layer-boundary framing (safety of writers, idempotency of readers): calling the idempotency/safety distinction a category error means neither 'safety of writers' nor 'idempotency of readers' is the correct characterization, while the layer-boundary view holds these are genuine, faithfully describable layer-bound obligations.","claim_class":"conflict","conflict_handles":["ART_3","ART_11"],"source_conflict_keys":["CNF_1"],"qualification":"ART_19, ART_22, ART_23, and ART_24 describe this contradiction and its potential refutation conditions."},{"entry_key":"CLM_18","claim":"Conflict between the 'safe writers required' position and the 'idempotent writers required' position: one camp holds that safety suffices because duplicate entries are tolerable if detectable during replay, while the other holds that deterministic replay demands byte-for-byte or semantic identity of the log regardless of retry count, requiring true idempotency.","claim_class":"conflict","conflict_handles":["ART_1","ART_4","ART_7","ART_9","ART_13","ART_18"],"source_conflict_keys":["CNF_2"]},{"entry_key":"CLM_19","claim":"The question of which property the append-only event log requires of its writers—idempotency, safety, or neither—is unresolved among competing positions, with an unresolved rivalry among 18 positions retained at formal sequence 676.","claim_class":"unknown","qualification":"The allowed sources (Python logging and sqlite3 documentation) do not contain published definitions of idempotency or safety in the distributed systems sense; the formal artifacts supply competing conjectures but no external adjudication is available within the bounded record."},{"entry_key":"CLM_20","claim":"Published definitions of idempotency and safety referenced by the formal artifacts—specifically RFC 9110 §9.2.1 and messaging literature (e.g., Burnstein & Shapiro on exactly-once semantics)—are not present in the catalog's allowed sources.","claim_class":"unknown"}],"source_conflicts":[{"conflict_key":"CNF_1","conflicting_handles":["ART_3","ART_11"],"description":"ART_11 (14e084ed5ca7) claims the idempotency/safety distinction is a category error for append-only log writers—the real invariant is protocol-level distinguishability via delivery metadata. ART_3 (2edd20cc5e66) claims safety is a genuine writer requirement via a layer-boundary distinction (safe writers, idempotent readers). Both share the mechanism of metadata-based distinguishability but contradict on whether calling a writer 'safe' is meaningful or a misnomer."},{"conflict_key":"CNF_2","conflicting_handles":["ART_1","ART_4","ART_7","ART_9","ART_13","ART_18"],"description":"ART_1, ART_7, and ART_13 claim the log requires safe (not idempotent) writers because append is non-idempotent but tolerates duplicates via dedup keys. ART_4, ART_9, and ART_18 claim the log requires idempotent writers because deterministic replay demands the replayed log be identical to the original, and safety permits state divergence. The disagreement turns on whether 'safe' tolerable duplicates suffice for deterministic replay or whether only true idempotency (identical log state regardless of retry count) satisfies it."}],"uncovered_requirements":[{"requirement":"Published definitions of idempotency and safety from distributed systems literature (RFC 9110 §9.2.1, Burnstein & Shapiro on exactly-once semantics) are referenced by formal artifacts but are not present in the catalog's allowed sources, which contain only Python logging and sqlite3 documentation.","reason":"The allowed source handles (SRC_1, SRC_2, SRC_3) and evidence handles (EVD_1, EVD_2, EVD_3) are Python standard library documentation pages that do not discuss idempotency or safety as distributed systems properties.","related_entry_keys":["CLM_9","CLM_19","CLM_20"]},{"requirement":"External adjudication among the 18 competing positions on whether an append-only event log requires idempotent writers, safe writers, or a different property entirely (protocol-level distinguishability, commutativity under dedup, effectively-idempotent composite operations, semilattice-fold processing).","reason":"The formal sequence retained an unresolved rivalry among 18 positions with no accepted resolution; the bounded record provides no source or evidence external to the formal artifacts that could adjudicate.","related_entry_keys":["CLM_17","CLM_18","CLM_19"]}]}
